$NetBSD$

Three things configure needs.

Machines and systems it has never heard of: alpha, powerpc and x86_64 on
NetBSD, and DragonFly and FreeBSD.

DragonFly and FreeBSD need an arm of their own in the outer case.  Setting
opsys further down is not enough on its own -- that only names the operating
system, and without a machine the configuration is rejected as unported.  The
machine rules cannot go in the NetBSD arm either, since that arm only runs
for a NetBSD triple.  src/s/dragonfly.h has been in this package since 2006
but nothing could reach it.

Its test for whether the C preprocessor works checks that nothing came out
on stderr.  A preprocessor that says anything at all -- a warning, a note --
fails the test even though it works.  Check that the output holds what the
input should have produced instead.

That check went into both arms of the test, but the second one still sent the
preprocessor's output to /dev/null while grepping conftest2.out, which the
`rm -rf conftest*' between the two arms has just removed.  Its verdict then
turned on whether grep could open a file rather than on whether
-traditional-cpp works, so either answer was an accident.  Point it at the
file it writes.

The last resort is /lib/cpp, which none of the systems this builds on has
had for decades.  config.status writes lib-src/Makefile and src/Makefile
through $CPP, so when it is not there both come out without rules and the
build dies much later with `No rule to make target "all"'.  That is what
DragonFly was doing.  Keep the -traditional-cpp candidate already in hand
unless /lib/cpp is really present.

The machine table has no x86_64 arm outside the BSDs, so configure stops with
"Emacs hasn't been ported to `x86_64--linux-gnu' systems" before it looks at
anything else.  m/amd64.h is already here and says nothing about the operating
system, so the arm is the same shape as the i386 one: pick the machine, then
let the inner case pick the opsys.

--- configure.orig
+++ configure
@@ -581,13 +581,33 @@
   *-*-netbsd* )
     opsys=netbsd
     case "${canonical}" in
+      alpha-*-netbsd*)	machine=alpha ;;
       i[345]86-*-netbsd*) machine=intel386 ;;
       m68k-*-netbsd*)
 			# This is somewhat bogus.
 			machine=hp9000s300 ;;
       mips-*-netbsd*)	machine=pmax ;;
       ns32k-*-netbsd*)	machine=ns32000 ;;
+      powerpc-*-netbsd*) machine=powerpc ;;
       sparc-*-netbsd*)	machine=sparc ;;
+      x86_64-*-netbsd*) machine=amd64 ;;
+    esac
+  ;;
+
+  ## DragonFly ports.  Only ever ran on x86_64.
+  *-*-dragonfly* )
+    opsys=dragonfly
+    case "${canonical}" in
+      x86_64-*-dragonfly*) machine=amd64 ;;
+    esac
+  ;;
+
+  ## FreeBSD ports
+  *-*-freebsd* )
+    opsys=freebsd
+    case "${canonical}" in
+      i[345]86-*-freebsd*) machine=intel386 ;;
+      x86_64-*-freebsd*) machine=amd64 ;;
     esac
   ;;
 
@@ -1177,11 +1197,21 @@
       *-bsd386* | *-bsdi* )	opsys=bsd386 ;;
       *-386bsd* )	        opsys=386bsd ;;
       *-freebsd* )	        opsys=freebsd ;;
+      *-dragonfly* )		opsys=dragonfly ;;
       *-nextstep* )             opsys=mach2 ;;
       ## Otherwise, we'll fall through to the generic opsys code at the bottom.
     esac
   ;;
 
+  ## x86_64 machines where we do not care about the manufacturer
+  x86_64-*-* )
+    machine=amd64
+    case "${canonical}" in
+      *-linux* )		opsys=linux ;;
+      ## Otherwise, we will fall through to the generic opsys code at the bottom.
+    esac
+  ;;
+
   * )
     unported=true
   ;;
@@ -1384,10 +1414,13 @@
 #include "confdefs.h"
 #include <stdio.h>
 Syntax Error
+Should \\
+be one line.
 EOF
 # Some shells (Coherent) do redirections in the wrong order, so need
 # the parens.
-ac_err=`eval "($ac_cpp conftest.${ac_ext} >/dev/null) 2>&1"`
+ac_err=`eval "($ac_cpp conftest.${ac_ext} > conftest2.out) 2>&1 ; \
+grep -L \"Should  *be one line\" conftest2.out"`
 if test -z "$ac_err"; then
   :
 else
@@ -1397,15 +1430,20 @@
 #include "confdefs.h"
 #include <stdio.h>
 Syntax Error
+Should \\
+be one line.
 EOF
 # Some shells (Coherent) do redirections in the wrong order, so need
 # the parens.
-ac_err=`eval "($ac_cpp conftest.${ac_ext} >/dev/null) 2>&1"`
+ac_err=`eval "($ac_cpp conftest.${ac_ext} > conftest2.out) 2>&1 ; \
+grep -L \"Should  *be one line\" conftest2.out"`
 if test -z "$ac_err"; then
   :
 else
   rm -rf conftest*
-  CPP=/lib/cpp
+  if test -x /lib/cpp; then
+    CPP=/lib/cpp
+  fi
 fi
 rm -f conftest*
 fi
